On Apr 16, 7:41 am, Rob Ellwood <rob.ellw...@shaw.ca.invalid> wrote:
> First, make it so that low level combat wombats will normally
> have zero spell points.  Give them zero spell points to start,
> when gaining a level, and even when quaffing Gain Energy.  Leave
> them at zero, barring unusual luck ("Your body absorbs part of
> the magical energy!") or deliberate effort (e.g.:  raise Wis,
> then level up).  This nerfs spellcasting for classes which don't
> depend on (and which rarely use) spells.

I'm pretty sure I don't like this, because it reduces the amount of
difference *between* the roles you do it to.  One of the things
that makes the Barbarian role significantly harder than a neutral
Valkyrie, for instance, is that Barbarians' innate spellcasting
penalty makes books effectively useless to them; whereas, Valkyries
*can* cast spells, it just takes a lot of doing to get there
(maxing out your helm of brilliance, getting rid of ALL metal).
This is still different from balanced roles (e.g., Tourist), who
can cast low-level spells nearly from the beginning of the game and
work their way up to mid-level spells without too much difficulty.

> Next, make applying a magic marker use Pw.  There are seven
> categories of base costs for writing scrolls.  OK, scrolls take
> [category #] Pw to write.  This is 5 for EA and EW:  easy for
> mages, acceptable for most others, and now a problem for combat
> wombats.  They will sometimes hit a big stumbling block at the
> castle.

All this would really do is force the player to wait a few dozen
turns between writing scrolls and using power for anything else
(e.g., teleporting) or vice versa.  Since most scroll-writing is
done in the middle game when food is plentiful, it adds more
tedious waiting but doesn't actually make anything more difficult.
I'm generally against changes that make the game more tedious
without making it more difficult.


